You need to find the rogue process that is writing messages noone is 
reading (or find why the reader went away), there is no other workaround 
or fix except for increasing the size of the message queues, which just 
delays the problem.

You can maybe get this info using the audit subsystem, here's an example 
I cut/pasted:

   # auditd -o o                                   ; to enable the auditd
   # auditmask -s cmd_name msgget msgsnd msgrcv    ; audit syscalls

After the message queue fills:

   # audit_tool `auditd -dq`            ; view data (note the csh syntax)

Which shows in this case the user with ruid 10 ran msgget_test which
exercised the msgget syscall:

   audit_id:    10            ruid/euid:   10/10
   pid:         656405        ppid: 656382               cttydev: (6,1)
   cmd name:    msgget_test
   event:       msgget
   key:         IPC_PRIVATE
   flag:        0x200 : creat
   id:          0x81
   result:      129 (0x81)
   cpu, seq#:   0x0, 1
   ip address:  xxx.xxx.xxx.xxx (xxxx)
   timestamp:   Mon Nov 24 11:45:02.51 2003 EST

If audit is not supported by your kernel, you can doconfig a new kernel
with DEC_AUDIT or run the "sysman auditconfig".

Manpages are available for each of the above utilities.  A "best 
practices" manual on Debugging with Audit is also available at:
   http://h30097.www3.hp.com/docs/best_practices/BP_AUDIT/TITLE.HTM

Good luck,

-Brian


